1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,600
Exoplanet Finder
A 1D CNN for classifying signals in NASA Kepler light curves

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,967
Kepler-90 i: a planet already known from published work, used here as a demonstration
Real Kepler light curve bundled with the app; the Kepler-90 star was held out from training

3
00:00:08,967 --> 00:00:13,433
Raw light curve: four years of Kepler brightness measurements
Kepler-90 i dims its star by only about 116 parts per million, invisible at this scale

4
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:20,100
Folding at the 14.45-day period stacks 74 observed transits on top of each other
The repeated dips line up, so the transit becomes visible (blue line: binned median)

5
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:25,933
Model input 1, the global view: the whole orbit in 2,001 bins
It shows structure away from the transit, such as eclipses of a companion star

6
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:31,867
Model input 2, the local view: a close-up of the dip in 201 bins
The 1D convolutional network reads both views together

7
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:37,167
Model score 0.987, above the validation-selected threshold of 0.671
The threshold was chosen on validation data, never on the test set

8
00:00:37,167 --> 00:00:41,367
Scores indicate resemblance to labelled planet candidates
Not a probability, and not a planet confirmation

9
00:00:41,367 --> 00:00:47,733
KIC 10587105 TCE 3 is labelled AFP (astrophysical false positive) in Kepler DR24
The model scores it 0.999, so the DR24-based benchmark counts it as an error

10
00:00:47,733 --> 00:00:52,333
Later DR25 vetting lists the matched KOI K00339.03 as CANDIDATE
NASA Exoplanet Archive, Planetary Systems table: confirmed planet Kepler-529 d

11
00:00:52,333 --> 00:00:56,600
Later-catalogue context only: the evaluation still uses the original DR24 labels
Matched by star, period and transit timing; catalogues checked 19 Nov 2026

12
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:02,633
Evaluation: 2,362 test signals from 1,481 held-out host stars
Splits are grouped by host star, so no star appears in both training and test

13
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:09,933
Average precision (AP) on the test set: CNN 0.913 vs gradient-boosting baseline 0.873
Precision–recall curve; random ranking would score about 0.23, the planet-candidate share

14
00:01:09,933 --> 00:01:12,533
Open source on GitHub
Code, trained model, evaluation and a beginner walkthrough

15
00:01:12,533 --> 00:01:18,333
Built by Muhammad Abu Bakr Siddique · Code, trained model and evaluation
https://github.com/mabsidcode/exoplanet-finder
Data: NASA Kepler mission, via MAST and the NASA Exoplanet Archive
Inspired by Shallue & Vanderburg (2018), AstroNet

